<h2>The design brief</h2>
<p>Parliament created Family Courts to secure speedy settlement of disputes relating to marriage and family affairs, with conciliation at the centre. Section 7 vests them with the jurisdiction of a district court in matrimonial suits — nullity, restitution, judicial separation, divorce, matrimonial property, legitimacy, guardianship and custody — and the magistrate\'s jurisdiction over maintenance under what is now Section 144 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023. One forum, two inherited jurisdictions, and a procedure rebuilt around five ideas.</p>

<h2>Five departures from ordinary procedure</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Provision</th><th>Ordinary court</th><th>Family Court</th></tr>
  <tr><td>s. 9 — duty to settle</td><td>Settlement is encouraged but optional</td><td>The court is bound, in the first instance, to assist and persuade the parties to arrive at a settlement, and may adjourn proceedings whenever settlement seems reasonably possible</td></tr>
  <tr><td>s. 11 — in camera</td><td>Open court is the rule</td><td>In camera on either party's desire, or on the court's own motion</td></tr>
  <tr><td>s. 12 / s. 5-6 — experts</td><td>Judge sits alone</td><td>Counsellors, medical and welfare experts and social welfare agencies are associated with the court's work</td></tr>
  <tr><td>s. 13 — representation</td><td>Right to counsel</td><td>No entitlement as of right; amicus assistance where justice requires</td></tr>
  <tr><td>ss. 14-16 — evidence</td><td>Evidence law applies with full rigour</td><td>Anything that assists the court may be received; substance-memoranda replace verbatim records; formal proof on affidavit</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What the settlement duty means in practice</h2>
<p>Section 9 is not a ritual first hearing. Delhi Family Courts refer parties to attached counsellors and to mediation centres at multiple stages, and a judge who sees a window for settlement mid-trial may lawfully pause the case for it. For litigants this has two consequences: come to early hearings with realistic settlement positions and authority to decide, and treat counselling sessions seriously — the counsellor\'s process is confidential in its conciliation function, but patterns of cooperation or obstruction are visible to the court. A settlement reached and recorded becomes a consent decree, which Section 19 then protects from appeal.</p>

<h2>Evidence: freedom with discipline</h2>
<p>The relaxation in Sections 14 to 16 is a tool for the judge, not a free-for-all for parties. Reports of counsellors, medical records, call records, photographs and electronic material can be received where they assist — but weight remains a judicial question, and the opposite party\'s right to respond to material relied upon is part of natural justice. Practically:</p>
<div class="check">
  <ul>
    <li>Prepare evidence as if ordinary standards applied — authentic documents, identified sources, proper certificates for electronic records — because weight follows reliability even where admissibility is relaxed.</li>
    <li>Use Section 15 to advantage: concise, well-structured examination produces a memorandum that actually reflects the case theory.</li>
    <li>Affidavit evidence of formal character under Section 16 saves hearing dates; reserve oral testimony for contested facts.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2>The appeal architecture</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Judgment or final order</strong> of the Family Court — in matrimonial and guardianship jurisdiction.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Section 19 appeal</strong> — to the High Court, Division Bench, within 30 days, on law and fact.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Exceptions</strong> — no appeal from consent decrees; maintenance orders under the criminal-procedure jurisdiction travel by revision, not appeal.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Interlocutory orders</strong> — outside Section 19; challenge, where at all, lies in the High Court's supervisory jurisdiction, sparingly exercised.</div>
</div>

<h2>Reading the forum right</h2>
<p>Parties and counsel who treat the Family Court as a civil court with a different nameplate consistently misjudge it: they litigate when the statute expects engagement with settlement, object on formal evidence grounds the statute has disabled, and burn goodwill before a judge whom the Act positions as problem-solver first and adjudicator second. The better approach works with the design — early, documented settlement efforts; clean, reliable evidence; measured conduct in counselling; and an eye, from day one, on how the record will read to the Division Bench if the case ends in a Section 19 appeal.</p>
<div class="note">
  <p>This article describes the general statutory scheme of the Family Courts Act, 1984, as applied in Delhi. It is information, not legal advice for any particular proceeding.</p>
</div>
